WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.946 --> 00:00:05.892
[EXCITING MUSIC]

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:16.400
The lands of the prehistoric planet

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:20.210
were ruled by the dinosaurs, but the seas

00:00:20.210 --> 00:00:22.070
dominated by a very different group

00:00:22.070 --> 00:00:25.070
of reptiles, the mosasaurs.

00:00:25.070 --> 00:00:27.950
And this is the skull of one of them.

00:00:33.860 --> 00:00:37.670
Mosasaurs were seagoing lizards.

00:00:37.670 --> 00:00:45.590
Think of a giant, swimming, whale-sized Komodo dragon.

00:00:45.590 --> 00:00:51.020
Tapered snout, rough skin, four fins instead of normal limbs,

00:00:51.020 --> 00:00:54.680
and a long tail that would look a lot like a shark tail

00:00:54.680 --> 00:00:57.330
but upside down.

00:00:57.330 --> 00:00:58.690
That's basically a mosasaur.

00:01:03.750 --> 00:01:09.360
The largest was mosasaurus hoffmanni.

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:12.090
It was an ambush predator.

00:01:12.090 --> 00:01:14.470
But what was the secret of its success?

00:01:18.740 --> 00:01:21.410
They can move from just still, not

00:01:21.410 --> 00:01:26.060
moving at all, to incredible speeds right before your eyes.

00:01:26.060 --> 00:01:28.340
In the modern world, we see this ability

00:01:28.340 --> 00:01:29.990
in reptiles like crocodiles.

00:01:29.990 --> 00:01:32.740
[SPLASHING]

00:01:38.480 --> 00:01:42.470
Their muscles can deliver short bursts of tremendous power.

00:01:46.330 --> 00:01:48.190
Mosasaurs given that they're reptiles,

00:01:48.190 --> 00:01:49.810
given that they're lizards, probably

00:01:49.810 --> 00:01:52.060
had similar capabilities in their muscles.

00:01:56.170 --> 00:02:00.330
They also had another adaptation that could increase

00:02:00.330 --> 00:02:03.680
the element of surprise.

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:05.810
One of the tricky things about swimming

00:02:05.810 --> 00:02:08.870
is you need a special kind of dynamic to get out of the gate

00:02:08.870 --> 00:02:12.050
quickly, to do the equivalent of a sprinter start

00:02:12.050 --> 00:02:12.980
from the blocks.

00:02:12.980 --> 00:02:14.780
And one of the best ways of doing that

00:02:14.780 --> 00:02:18.590
is to bend yourself into a C shape

00:02:18.590 --> 00:02:22.330
and then push off of the water of the side of your body.

00:02:22.330 --> 00:02:25.600
Fish today use this technique.

00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:28.630
Known as a sea start, it allows them

00:02:28.630 --> 00:02:34.880
to accelerate from a standstill to full speed in an instant.

00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:38.270
Mosasaurs likely were able to do something very similar

00:02:38.270 --> 00:02:40.025
but, of course, at a much grander scale.

00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:49.580
So exactly how fast could a giant

00:02:49.580 --> 00:02:53.960
like mosasaurus hoffmanni actually swim?

00:02:53.960 --> 00:02:57.170
To find out, the Prehistoric Planet team

00:02:57.170 --> 00:03:00.275
commissioned a unique scientific study.

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:05.310
Until recently, no one had really

00:03:05.310 --> 00:03:08.970
attempted to estimate these kinds of performance values

00:03:08.970 --> 00:03:10.290
in detail.

00:03:10.290 --> 00:03:12.090
Our work is one of the first attempts

00:03:12.090 --> 00:03:16.320
to really put numbers to the abilities of these animals.

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:19.605
And the results we got from that are really pretty stunning.

00:03:22.850 --> 00:03:25.670
The team ran the calculations four times

00:03:25.670 --> 00:03:27.035
to ensure they were correct.

00:03:29.690 --> 00:03:31.610
All four of the different trials came

00:03:31.610 --> 00:03:34.730
to the same overall conclusion.

00:03:34.730 --> 00:03:40.560
These animals were able to generate massive accelerations.

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:43.500
[SPLASH]

00:03:56.250 --> 00:03:58.560
This mosasaur could probably clear

00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:03.090
75% of its body length in one single second.

00:04:03.090 --> 00:04:08.340
That means if that mosasaur is 17 meters away from you, in one

00:04:08.340 --> 00:04:11.310
second it will be 75% closer.

00:04:11.310 --> 00:04:12.780
And in the second second, it would

00:04:12.780 --> 00:04:15.240
have surpassed you and probably eaten you along the way.

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:20.640
Being hit by a mosasaurus would be

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:24.180
a bit like being hit by a full size semi truck.

00:04:24.180 --> 00:04:26.850
Just the impact of the animal alone,

00:04:26.850 --> 00:04:29.670
much less the bite that would follow

00:04:29.670 --> 00:04:32.700
would kill a lot of its prey instantly.

00:04:36.790 --> 00:04:39.310
Mosasaurs could reach 30 miles an hour

00:04:39.310 --> 00:04:41.340
in as little as one second.

00:04:45.390 --> 00:04:50.330
This is what makes them arguably the ultimate marine predator

00:04:50.330 --> 00:04:51.800
of all time.

00:04:51.800 --> 00:04:54.850
[EXCITING MUSIC]

